From 75c92988dd4a6975f94998d9f28a8cbd865234fd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bram Cohen <bram@chia.net>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 19:14:21 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Break ties in Boolean3 with one consistent perturbation

Boolean3 decides a tie between P and Q (two values within TieTol) by a
direction per check: vertex normals for x, the sum of an edge's face
normals for y, face normals for z. These don't describe one perturbed
configuration, and where the direction is exactly 0, which a face normal
component of 0 makes common in axis-aligned and grid-aligned input, the
check answers the same both ways. The checks then contradict each other.
Batch unions of convex pieces with coplanar faces (the sweep of a voxel
shape's faces by a cube, a Minkowski sum) are left with zero-volume flaps
of overlapping triangles, depending on how the normals in memory happen
to round.

Every tie is now decided as it would come out in a single perturbed
configuration:

1. Each vertex moves by an infinitesimal along a displacement, outward
   for Q and for P when expanding, inward for P otherwise. Where a vertex
   touches at most three distinct face planes, the displacement moves all
   of them outward by the same distance, so flat faces stay flat and
   faces of P and Q that coincide stay tied. Elsewhere it is the vertex
   normal.
2. P moves once more by a smaller infinitesimal, so that where P's and
   Q's faces coincide, P's is the outer one on every side.
3. P is translated along +x, then +y, then +z by successively smaller
   infinitesimals, which settles anything left.

At a tie, the y, edge-edge and vertex-face comparisons are signs of a 2D
cross product or a 3x3 determinant of vertex differences divided by
another: polynomials of degree at most 3 in those infinitesimals, whose
sign is that of the most significant nonzero coefficient, computed lazily.
Where the divisor is exactly 0 (an edge perpendicular to x, edges parallel
in projection, a face parallel to z), its sign is taken from the
perturbation as well; the earlier per-edge or per-face rule remains only
where the perturbation leaves it 0 too.

Shadows is no longer used and is removed.

Tests: CoplanarBatchUnion and CollinearEdgesBatchUnion compare a batch
union of swept face pieces with the same union taken one piece at a time;
the first fails on master, the second with the per-edge rule for edges
parallel in projection. CubeUnionProp and MeshGLRoundTrip go back to 18
verts / 32 tris: their coplanar faces come from different meshes, so the
corner of the upper one stays a vertex of the seam. Simplify's
RemoveDegenerates count is 34.

